  "body": "Trimming a graph today forces a choice: stream-copy and land on the nearest\nkeyframe (off by up to a GOP), or re-encode the whole stream and lose a\ngeneration. This branch adds a third option — re-encode only the boundary,\ncopy everything else byte-for-byte.\n\n## Video — `codec_video: \"smartcopy\"`\n\nA new `graph.KindSmartCopy` node sits between source and sink. It classifies\nGOPs against the trim window, copies whole interior GOPs verbatim, and decodes\n+ re-encodes only the GOP(s) the cut points land in. Boundary decode is primed\nwith the previous GOP so open-GOP references resolve. Target inherits the\nsource codec, size, frame rate, pixel format, SAR, profile/level and bit rate.\n\nSupporting changes: the source stays a plain demuxer forwarding the raw\nbitstream; the sink registers via `AddStreamFromInput` (extradata from source)\nand skips output-timing on the smartcopy channel; a smartcopy output never\nfires a global `-to` stop, so the tail GOP can complete.\n\n## Audio — sample-accurate on both paths\n\n- **Re-encoded audio** (`spliceAudioTrimForOutputs`): inserts an `atrim` in\n  front of the audio encoder, mirroring FFmpeg's `insert_trim` in fftools.\n  Cuts now land on the exact sample rather than the nearest packet —\n  previously off by up to ~21 ms per edge. No `asetpts`; the sink's existing\n  shift-to-zero handles rebasing.\n- **PCM** (`codec_audio: \"smartcopy\"`): a per-packet (not per-GOP) state\n  machine copies interior packets verbatim and byte-slices the packets\n  straddling each edge to the exact sample. No decode, no encoder priming, no\n  container edit-list surgery — lossless and sample-exact.\n\nCompressed audio (AAC/FLAC/Opus/…) is rejected with an error pointing at\n`codec_audio: <encoder>` or `codec_audio: \"copy\"`. Seamless boundary\nre-encode there needs per-codec priming and gapless handling (pre-skip, edit\nlists) and is deferred.\n\n## GUI\n\n\"Smart copy (video)\" and \"Smart copy (audio, PCM)\" palette entries, each with\na dedicated properties panel. The video panel exposes the full boundary\nencoder (rate control, preset, tune, profile/level, raw x264/x265-params) plus\na boundary-encoder override and global-header toggle. The audio panel is a\nno-parameter info panel — PCM slicing has no encoder. The trim window lives on\nthe connected output's Timing section, since it applies to every stream.\n\nRound-trip: `expandImplicitEncoders` skips explicit smartcopy nodes so\nGUI-authored graphs aren't re-wrapped, and a new `stampSmartCopyTiming` pass\ncopies the window from the output options onto explicit nodes.\n\n## Testing\n\n- `job/handlers_smartcopy_test.go` — interior packets byte-identical to the\n  source on an open-GOP input; only boundary GOPs re-encoded.\n- `job/smart_audio_copy_test.go` — a fractional-second PCM trim is bit-for-bit\n  equal to the source sample sub-range.\n- `job/audio_trim_test.go` — re-encoded audio trim lands within ~13 ms\n  (sub-frame) of the requested window on the BBB fixture.\n- `TestSmartCopyExplicitNode` — GUI-shaped graph (explicit node, window in\n  output options, codec shorthand stripped) produces a byte-identical interior.\n- `testdata/examples/67_smartcopy_trim.json` runs end-to-end in\n  `TestExamplesRun`.\n\n`go build ./...` and `go test ./...` are clean.\n\n## Docs\n\nNew `docs/smartcopy.md`; updates to `README.md`, `docs/gui.md`,\n`docs/json-config-reference.md`, `CHANGELOG.md`. Embedded GUI assets rebuilt\nvia `make build-gui-static`.\n\n## Also included\n\n`chore(deps)` bumping the golang.org/x modules, which also restores `go.sum`\n`/go.mod` hash lines that had gone missing and broke the build.\n",
